#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151824 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152270 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d510b2f-df38-5fbe-a420-4ea79135488c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Constant of Proportionality"}] :question/id #uuid "e85107d6-ecdf-526d-a7ce-091d028039eb" :question/math-academy-id "e-13098" :question/problem "Adam started writing a book. There is a proportional relationship between the number of pages $x$ he writes and the book's thickness $y$ (in millimeters). Adam measured the thickness of his book to be $3$ millimeters after he'd written $18$ pages.\n\nWhat is the constant of proportionality for the relationship between $x$ and $y$?" :question/worked-solution "We are told that after Adam wrote $x=18$ pages, the book's thickness was $y=3$ millimeters.\n\nWe can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-value to the $x$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{y}{x} = \\dfrac{3}{18} = \\dfrac{3 \\div 3}{18 \\div 3} = \\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nand its units are \"millimeters per page.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151825 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152271 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a9d0449-2b61-5979-bd5c-94c33c8899c2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Equation of a Proportional Relationship"}] :question/id #uuid "15f20ecd-503f-5748-b004-680b5fc127c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-13099" :question/problem "Beatrice needs to clean the garage. So, to motivate herself, she allows herself $10$ minutes of video game time for every $25$ minutes she spends cleaning.\n\nThere is a proportional relationship between the time $x$ (in minutes) Beatrice spends cleaning the garage and the time $y$ (in minutes) she plays video games. What equation represents the relationship between $x$ and $y$?" :question/worked-solution "Every proportional relationship takes the form $y=kx$, where $k$ is the constant of proportionality.\n\nWe are told that for every $x=25$ minutes Beatrice spends cleaning, she will play $y=10$ minutes of video games.\n\nWe can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-value to the $x$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{y}{x} = \\dfrac{10}{25} = \\dfrac{10 \\div 5}{25 \\div 5} = \\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{2}{5} x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151826 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152272 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f29a835-91e3-51e3-a016-e13d296b7654" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Amount"}] :question/id #uuid "6b17572d-a8f7-523f-9697-ae16f3d84398" :question/math-academy-id "e-13100" :question/problem "Amy is baking a loaf of bread. If the loaf weighs $12$ ounces, then Amy needs to bake the loaf for $40$ minutes. There is a proportional relationship between the loaf's weight (in ounces) and the time (in minutes) the loaf needs to bake.\n\nIf Amy leaves the house for $60$ minutes and wants the loaf to be fully baked the moment she returns, how much should the loaf of bread weigh?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the loaf's weight (in ounces), and $y$ be the time (in minutes) the loaf needs to bake.\n\nFirst, we need to construct the equation that represents the proportional relationship. Every proportional relationship takes the form $y=kx$, where $k$ is the constant of proportionality.\n\nWe are told that if the loaf weighs $x=12$ ounces, then Amy needs to bake it for $y=40$ minutes. We can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-value to the $x$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{y}{x} = \\dfrac{40}{12} = \\dfrac{40 \\div 4}{12 \\div 4} = \\dfrac{10}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{10}{3} x\n$$\n\nNow, we want to find the value of $x$ so that the loaf will bake for exactly $y=60$ minutes. So, we substitute $y=60$ into the equation above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n60 &= \\frac{10}{3}x \\\\\n\\frac{10}{3}x &= 60\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve for $x$, we multiply both sides of the equation by the reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10}{3}\n$$\n\nwhich is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{10} \\cdot \\frac{10}{3}x &= \\frac{3}{10} \\cdot 60 \\\\\n1x &= \\frac{3 \\cdot 60}{10} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{180}{10} \\\\\nx &= 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the loaf of bread should weigh $18$ ounces."}]}
